{:edb/db-before-t 132 :edb/db-after-t 133 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533445 50 #inst "2026-10-10T22:30:07.435Z" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186054371 1071 "Consider the following statement:\n\n$14$ is divisible by $4$ **or** $\\sqrt{3}$ is a rational number.\n\nWrite this statement in symbolic form using the following notations:\n\n- ![](images/43/437d6f4601c7ccb76c02bdffecd0079dd3beb5bb4faf41c2d3074617b8a3b069.png)\n\n- ![](images/10/10bd9b53b9b98734e5e1052927d77f02b46556e61e51663de34e66b303edfc9e.png)" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186054371 1071 "Consider the following statement:\n\n> $14$ is divisible by $4$ **or** $\\sqrt{3}$ is a rational number.\n\nWrite this statement in symbolic form using the following notations:\n\n- $14$\n- $\\sqrt{3}$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186054371 1074 "The word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$\\lor$\".\n\nSo, the sentence reads\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{14 \\vphantom{p}\\textrm{ is divisible by }\\vphantom{p} 4 }_S \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textbf{or}}_{\\lor} \\quad \\underbrace{\\sqrt{3} \\vphantom{p}\\textrm{ is a rational number} }_T,\n$$\n\nthat is, $S \\lor T.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186054371 1074 "The word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$\\lor$\".\n\nSo, the sentence reads\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{14 \\vphantom{p}\\textrm{is divisible by}\\vphantom{p} 4}_S \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textbf{or}}_{\\lor} \\quad \\underbrace{\\sqrt{3} \\vphantom{p}\\textrm{is a rational number}}_T\n$$\n\nthat is, $S \\lor T$." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186054372 1071 "$7$ is odd **or** a multiple of $3$, **and** $3$ is less than $7$.\n\nExpress the compound statement above in symbolic form given the following notations:\n\n- ![](images/63/6378f3d805dd0595262cbd3a955fa0b2b74f72dcef9e3384eaced510d4bc58fd.png)\n\n- ![](images/9e/9eebe764937f6efdf47efc63912838592ed023d0b42b670e0f83b5d9d837e41b.png)\n\n- ![](images/17/17cb303f80bb1b44c30b4c64e1101523b555cc34158f160dc68fd5f81826070e.png)" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186054372 1071 "> $7$ is odd **or** a multiple of $3$, **and** $3$ is less than $7$.\n\nExpress the compound statement above in symbolic form given the following notations:\n\n- $7$\n- $7$\n- $3$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186054372 1074 "The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$\\land$\", while the word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$\\lor$\".\n\nNote that this sentence consists of two parts:\n\n- **Part 1:** $7$ is odd or a multiple of $3$. This corresponds to\n$\\underbrace{7 \\vphantom{p}\\textrm{is odd}}_P \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textbf{or}}_{\\lor} \\quad \\underbrace{7 \\vphantom{p}\\textrm{is a multiple of}3}_Q$,\nthat is, $P \\lor Q$.\n- **Part 2:** $3$ is less than $7$. This corresponds to $R$.\n\nSo, we can express the sentence as\n$\\underbrace{7 \\vphantom{p}\\textrm{is odd} \\textbf{or} \\vphantom{p} \\vphantom{p}\\textrm{a multiple of} 3}_{P \\, \\lor \\,Q}, \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textbf{and}}_{\\land} \\quad \\underbrace{3 \\vphantom{p}\\textrm{is less than} 7}_R$\n\nthat is, $(P \\lor Q) \\land R$." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186054372 1074 "The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$\\land$\", while the word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$\\lor$\".\n\nNote that this sentence consists of two parts:\n\n- **Part 1:** $7$ is odd or a multiple of $3.$ This corresponds to\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{7 \\vphantom{p}\\textrm{ is odd }}_P \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textbf{or}}_{\\lor} \\quad \\underbrace{7 \\vphantom{p}\\textrm{ is a multiple of }3}_Q,\n$$\n\nthat is, $P \\lor Q.$\n\n- **Part 2:** $3$ is less than $7$. This corresponds to $R.$\n\nSo, we can express the sentence as\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{7 \\vphantom{p}\\textrm{ is odd} \\textbf{ or } \\vphantom{p} \\vphantom{p}\\textrm{ a multiple of } 3}_{P \\, \\lor \\,Q}, \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textbf{and}}_{\\land} \\quad \n\\underbrace{3 \\vphantom{p}\\textrm{ is less than } 7 }_R\n$$\n\nthat is, $(P \\lor Q) \\land R.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186054374 1071 "Consider the following compound statement:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{5 \\vphantom{p}\\textrm{ is prime} }_A \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textbf{or}}_{\\lor} \\quad \\underbrace{\\dfrac13 \\in \\mathbb{Z}}_B\n$$\n\nFind the truth values of the statements $A$ and $B,$ and the compound statement $A\\lor B.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186054374 1071 "Consider the following compound statement:\n\n$\\underbrace{5 \\vphantom{p}\\textrm{is prime}}_A \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textbf{or}}_{\\lor} \\quad \\underbrace{\\dfrac13 \\in \\mathbb{Z}}_B$\n\nFind the truth values of the statements $A$ and $B$, and the compound statement $A\\lor B$." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186054374 1074 "First, let's recall the following facts about the \"and\" $(\\land)$ and \"or\" $(\\lor)$ logical connectives:\n\n- The statement $A \\land B$ is true if *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n- The statement $A \\lor B$ is true if either $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\nIn our case, $A$ is true, and $B$ is false. Therefore, the result is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{5\\text{ is prime}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{\\frac{1}{3}\\in Z}_{⏟}}_{B} & \\,\\,≡\\,\\text{true}∨\\text{false}≡\\text{true}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186054374 1074 "First, let's recall the following facts about the \"and\" $(\\land)$ and \"or\" $(\\lor)$ logical connectives:\n\n- The statement $A \\land B$ is true if *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n- The statement $A \\lor B$ is true if either $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\nIn our case, $A$ is true, and $B$ is false. Therefore, the result is true:\n\n$$\n5pis prime_(⏟)_(A)por_(⏟)_(∨)\\frac{1}{3} ∈ Z_(⏟)_(B) \\mid ≡true∨false≡true\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186054395 1071 "Consider the following compound statement:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\mathbb{Z}\\cup \\mathbb{R}=\\mathbb{Z} }_P \n\\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{|}\\textbf{and}}_{\\land} \\quad \\underbrace{\\overbrace{7 \\,|\\, 21}^Q \n\\quad \\overbrace{\\vphantom{|}\\textbf{or}}^{\\lor} \\quad \n\\overbrace{7 \\,|\\, 29}^R }_{Q \\, \\lor \\, R}\n$$\n\nFind the truth values of the statements $P,$ $Q$ and $R,$ and the compound statements $Q\\lor R$ and $P\\land (Q\\lor R).$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186054395 1071 "Consider the following compound statement:\n\n$\\underbrace{\\mathbb{Z}\\cup \\mathbb{R}=\\mathbb{Z}}_P \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{\\mid}\\textbf{and}}_{\\land} \\quad \\underbrace{\\overbrace{7 \\, \\mid \\, 21}^Q \\quad \\overbrace{\\vphantom{\\mid}\\textbf{or}}^{\\lor} \\quad \\overbrace{7 \\, \\mid \\, 29}^R}_{Q \\, \\lor \\, R}$\n\nFind the truth values of the statements $P$, $Q$ and $R$, and the compound statements $Q\\lor R$ and $P\\land (Q\\lor R)$." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186054395 1074 "First, let's recall the following facts about the \"and\" $(\\land)$ and \"or\" $(\\lor)$ logical connectives:\n\n- The statement $A \\land B$ is true if *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n- The statement $A \\lor B$ is true if either $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\nIn our case, $P$ is false, $Q$ is true, and $R$ is false. Therefore, $Q \\lor R$ is true, but the result is false:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P∧(Q∨R) & ≡\\text{false}∧\\overset{\\text{true}}{\\overset{⏞}{(\\text{true}∨\\text{false})}} \\\\  & ≡\\text{false}∧\\text{true} \\\\  & ≡\\text{false}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186054395 1074 "First, let's recall the following facts about the \"and\" $(\\land)$ and \"or\" $(\\lor)$ logical connectives:\n\n- The statement $A \\land B$ is true if *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n- The statement $A \\lor B$ is true if either $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\nIn our case, $P$ is false, $Q$ is true, and $R$ is false. Therefore, $Q \\lor R$ is true, but the result is false:\n\n$$\nP∧(Q∨R) \\mid ≡false∧(true∨false)^(⏞)^(true); ≡false∧true; ≡false\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186054452 1071 "Find $\\mathcal{P}(A)$ if $A = \\{ 2, 4, \\{ 8 \\} \\}.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186054452 1071 "Find $\\mathcal{P}(A)$ if $A = \\{2, 4, \\{8 \\} \\}$." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186054452 1074 "The power set of $A$, denoted $\\mathcal{P}(A)$, is the set that contains every possible subset of $A$, including the empty set $\\emptyset$.\n\nSo, let's list all possible subsets of $A$ using a tree diagram.\n\n![](images/b3/b3af256806e0aeb0396564320800f908c66d5830ab46e676dfc51ceab24154f6.png)\n\nAccording to our diagram, the subsets of $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\emptyset = \\{\\},\\quad \\{2\\},\\quad \\{4\\},\\quad \\{\\{8\\} \\}, \\quad \\{2, 4\\},\\quad \\{2, \\{8\\}\\},\\quad \\{4, \\{8\\}\\},\\quad\\{2, 4, \\{8\\}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{P}(A) = \\{\\emptyset, \\{2\\}, \\{4\\}, \\{\\{8\\}\\}, \\{2, 4\\}, \\{2, \\{8\\}\\}, \\{4, \\{8\\}\\},\\{2, 4, \\{8\\}\\}\\}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186054452 1074 "The power set of $A,$ denoted $\\mathcal{P}(A),$ is the set that contains every possible subset of $A,$ including the empty set $\\emptyset.$\n\nSo, let's list all possible subsets of $A$ using a tree diagram.\n\n![](images/b3/b3af256806e0aeb0396564320800f908c66d5830ab46e676dfc51ceab24154f6.png)\n\nAccording to our diagram, the subsets of $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\emptyset = \\{\\},\\quad \\{2\\},\\quad \\{4\\},\\quad \\{ \\{8\\} \\}, \\quad \\{2, 4\\},\\quad \\{2, \\{8\\}\\},\\quad \\{4, \\{8\\}\\},\\quad\\{2, 4, \\{8\\}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{P}(A) = \\{\\emptyset, \\{2\\}, \\{4\\}, \\{\\{8\\}\\}, \\{2, 4\\}, \\{2, \\{8\\}\\}, \\{4, \\{8\\}\\},\\{2, 4, \\{8\\}\\}\\}.\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186054453 1071 "If $Y = \\{ 1, \\{ 3, 5 \\}, 7, X \\},$ then what is $\\left|\\mathcal{P}(Y)\\right|?$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186054453 1071 "If $Y = \\{1, \\{3, 5 \\}, 7, X \\}$, then what is $\\left|\\mathcal{P}(Y)\\right|$?" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186054453 1074 "For any set $Y$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left|\\mathcal{P}(Y)\\right| = 2^{\\left| Y \\right|}\n$$\n\nIn this case,\n\n$$\n\\left| Y \\right| = 4\n$$\n\nso we have\n\n$$\n\\left| \\mathcal{P}(Y) \\right| = 2^{\\left| Y \\right|} = 2^{4} = 16\n$$\n\nRemember that $\\{3,5 \\}$ is a single element in the set $Y$." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186054453 1074 "For any set $Y,$ we have $\\left|\\mathcal{P}(Y)\\right| = 2^{\\left| Y \\right|}.$\n\nIn this case, $\\left| Y \\right| = 4,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\left| \\mathcal{P}(Y) \\right| = 2^{\\left| Y \\right|} = 2^{4} = 16.\n$$\n\nRemember that $\\{ 3,5 \\}$ is a single element in the set $Y.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186054454 1071 "Given that $Y =\\{\\mu\\}$ and $Z =\\{1,2\\}$, find $\\left|\\mathcal{P}(\\mathcal{P}(Y\\times Z))\\right|$." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186054454 1071 "Given that $Y =\\{\\mu\\}$ and $Z =\\{1,2\\},$ find $\\left|\\mathcal{P}(\\mathcal{P}(Y\\times Z))\\right|.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186054454 1074 "For any set $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left|\\mathcal{P}(S)\\right| = 2^{\\left| S \\right|}\n$$\n\nAlso,\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}S \\times T & = & S & \\cdot & T\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\left| Y \\right| = 1, \\qquad \\left| Z \\right| = 2\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}P(Y \\cdot Z) & &= 2^{\\mid Y \\cdot Z\\end{vmatrix}} \\\\\n&= 2^{\\begin{vmatrix}Y & \\cdot & Z\\end{vmatrix}} \\\\\n&= 2^{1 \\cdot 2} \\\\\n&= 2^{2} \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}P(P(Y \\cdot Z)) & &= 2^{\\mid P(Y \\cdot Z)\\end{vmatrix}} \\\\\n&= 2^{4} \\\\\n&= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186054454 1074 "For any set $S,$ we have $\\left|\\mathcal{P}(S)\\right| = 2^{\\left| S \\right|}.$ Also, $|S \\times T| = |S| \\cdot |T|.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\left| Y \\right| = 1, \\qquad\n\\left| Z \\right| = 2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|P(Y\\times Z)| & ={2}^{|Y\\times Z|} \\\\  & ={2}^{|Y|\\,\\cdot \\,|Z|} \\\\  & ={2}^{1\\cdot 2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|P(P(Y\\times Z))| & ={2}^{|P(Y\\times Z)|} \\\\  & ={2}^{4} \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186054455 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathcal{P}(\\{\\{\\emptyset\\}\\})  = \\{ \\emptyset, \\{\\emptyset\\} \\}$\n\n- $\\mathcal{P} (\\{ \\emptyset, \\{0\\} \\} ) = \\{ \\emptyset, \\{0\\}, \\{\\{\\emptyset\\}\\}, \\{0, \\emptyset\\} \\}$\n\n- $\\mathcal{P}( \\mathcal{P}(\\{y\\}))  = \\{ \\emptyset, \\{\\emptyset\\}, \\{\\{y\\}\\}, \\{\\emptyset, \\{y\\}\\} \\}$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186054455 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\mathcal{P}(\\{\\{\\emptyset\\}\\}) = \\{\\emptyset, \\{\\emptyset\\} \\}$\n2. $\\mathcal{P} (\\{\\emptyset, \\{0\\} \\}) = \\{\\emptyset, \\{0\\}, \\{\\{\\emptyset\\}\\}, \\{0, \\emptyset\\} \\}$\n3. $\\mathcal{P}(\\mathcal{P}(\\{y\\})) = \\{\\emptyset, \\{\\emptyset\\}, \\{\\{y\\}\\}, \\{\\emptyset, \\{y\\}\\} \\}$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186054455 1074 "The power set of $T$, denoted $\\mathcal{P}(T)$, is the set that contains every possible subset of $T$, including the empty set $\\emptyset$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that we are given the set $\\{\\{\\emptyset\\}\\}$. So, its power set must be $\\{\\emptyset, \\{\\{\\emptyset\\}\\} \\}$.\n- Statement II is false. Notice that we are given the set $\\{\\emptyset, \\{0\\} \\}$. So, its power set must be $\\{\\emptyset, \\{\\{0\\}\\}, \\{\\emptyset\\}, \\{\\emptyset, \\{0\\} \\} \\}$.\n- Statement III is true. Notice that we are given the set $\\{y\\}$. So, its power set must be $\\{\\emptyset, \\{y\\} \\}$. Therefore, $\\mathcal{P}(\\mathcal{P}(\\{y\\})) = \\{\\emptyset, \\{\\emptyset\\}, \\{\\{y\\}\\}, \\{\\emptyset, \\{y\\}\\} \\}$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186054455 1074 "The power set of $T,$ denoted $\\mathcal{P}(T),$ is the set that contains every possible subset of $T,$ including the empty set $\\emptyset.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that we are given the set $\\{\\{\\emptyset\\}\\}.$ So, its power set must be $\\{ \\emptyset, \\{\\{\\emptyset\\}\\} \\}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that we are given the set $\\{ \\emptyset, \\{0\\} \\} .$ So, its power set must be $\\{ \\emptyset, \\{\\{0\\}\\}, \\{\\emptyset\\}, \\{\\emptyset, \\{0\\} \\} \\}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that we are given the set $\\{y\\}.$ So, its power set must be $\\{\\emptyset, \\{y\\} \\}.$ Therefore, $\\mathcal{P}( \\mathcal{P}(\\{y\\})) = \\{ \\emptyset, \\{\\emptyset\\}, \\{\\{y\\}\\}, \\{\\emptyset, \\{y\\}\\} \\}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186054643 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362538 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186054643 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362737 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186054643 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364420 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186054644 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362545 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186054644 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362552 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186054644 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362723 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186054644 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362730 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186054644 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364427 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186