's examine our sets.\n\n- Set I is infinite since the underlying set $N$ is infinite.\n\n- Set II is finite since the underlying set is finite. Notice that \n\n$$\nP({Z,\\pi })={∅,\\,{Z},\\,{\\pi },\\,{Z,\\pi }}\n$$\n\nhas cardinality $4.$\n\n- Set III is finite since the underlying set is finite. Notice that \n\n$$\n{x\\in Z∣\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0}={0},\n$$\n\nand, as a result, \n\n$$\nP({x\\in Z∣\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0})=P({0})={∅,\\,{0}}\n$$\n\nhas cardinality $2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186054984 1068 #edb/ref 17592186051404 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186054984 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362717 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186054984 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186054984 1071 "If $T = {r, s, t}$, then $P(T) =$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186054984 1071 "If $T={r,s,t},$ then $P(T)=$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186054984 1074 "The power set of $T,$ denoted $P(T),$ is the set that contains every possible subset of $T,$ including the empty set $∅.$\n\nSo, let's list all possible subsets of $T$ using a tree diagram.\n\n![](images/91/916d0207e217da90b6421244f0f02d59d8f9dbafc2a7045033eab5ae2b69740e.png)\n\nAccording to our diagram, the subsets of $T$ are\n\n$$\n∅={},\\,{r},\\,{s},\\,{t},\\,{r,s},\\,{r,t},\\,{s,t},\\,{r,s,t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nP(T)={∅,{r},{s},{t},{r,s},{r,t},{s,t},{r,s,t}}.\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186055044 1068 #edb/ref 17592186051764 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186055044 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363264 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186055044 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186055044 1071 "$7$ is prime **or** divisible by $10,$ **and** $5$ is rational **or** less than $100.$\n\nExpress the compound statement above in symbolic form given the following notations:\n\n- $P:7\\text{ is prime}$\n\n- $D:7\\text{ is divisible by }10$\n\n- $Q:5\\text{ is rational}$\n\n- $L:5\\text{ is less than }100$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186055044 1071 "> $7$ is prime **or** divisible by $10$, **and** $5$ is rational **or** less than $100$.\n\nExpress the compound statement above in symbolic form given the following notations:\n\n- $P: 7is prime$\n- $D: 7is divisible by10$\n- $Q: 5is rational$\n- $L: 5is less than100$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186055044 1074 "The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$∧$\", while the word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$∨$\".\n\nNote that this sentence consists of two parts:\n\n- **Part 1:** $7$ is prime or divisible by $10.$ This corresponds to\n\n$$\n{{7\\text{ is prime}}_{⏟}}_{P}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{7\\text{ is divisible by }10}_{⏟}}_{D}\n$$\n\nthat is, $P∨D.$\n\n- **Part 2:** $5$ is rational or less than $100.$ This corresponds to\n\n$$\n{{5\\text{ is rational}}_{⏟}}_{Q}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{5\\text{ is less than }100}_{⏟}}_{L},\n$$\n\nthat is, $Q∨L.$\n\nSo, we can express the sentence as\n\n$$\n{{7\\text{ is prime }\\text{or}\\text{ divisible by }10}_{⏟}}_{P\\,∨\\,D}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{5\\text{ is rational }\\text{or}\\text{ less than }100}_{⏟}}_{Q\\,∨\\,L},\n$$\n\nthat is, $(P∨D)∧(Q∨L).$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186055094 1068 #edb/ref 17592186051980 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186055094 1068 #edb/ref 17592186051983 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186055094 1068 #edb/ref 17592186051986 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186055094 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363392 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186055094 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363395 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186055094 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363398 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186055094 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186055094 1071 "$$\n{{2\\text{ is prime}}_{⏟}}_{R}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{\\overset{S}{\\overset{⏞}{7\\,|\\,14}}\\,\\overset{∧}{\\overset{⏞}{\\text{and}}}\\,\\overset{T}{\\overset{⏞}{5\\,|\\,14}}}_{⏟}}_{S\\,∧\\,T}\n$$\n\nFor the compound statement above, we have that\n\n$\\,$ $R$ is {{field-1}},\n$\\,$ $S∧T$ is {{field-2}},\n$\\,$ $R∨(S∧T)$ is {{field-3}}." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186055094 1071 "$2\\begin{vmatrix}is prime_(⏟)_(R) & or_(⏟)_(∨)7 & 14^(⏞)^(S) & and^(⏞)^(∧)5\\end{vmatrix}14^(⏞)^(T)_(⏟)_(S∧T)$\n\nFor the compound statement above, we have that\n\n$R$ is {{field-1}},\n$S∧T$ is {{field-2}},\n$R∨(S∧T)$ is {{field-3}}." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186055094 1074 "First, let's recall the following facts about the \"and\" $(∧)$ and \"or\" $(∨)$ logical connectives:\n\n- The statement $A∧B$ is true if *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n- The statement $A∨B$ is true if either $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\nIn our case, $R$ is true, $S$ is true, and $T$ is false. Therefore, $S∧T$ is false, but the result is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R∨(S∧T) & ≡\\text{true}∨\\overset{\\text{false}}{\\overset{⏞}{(\\text{true}∧\\text{false})}} \\\\  & ≡\\text{true}∨\\text{false} \\\\  & ≡\\text{true}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186059415 1074 "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A{:}$ You take an umbrella.\n\n- $B{:}$ You will stay dry.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A \\Rightarrow B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\neg A \\lor B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $\\neg A{:}$ You do not take an umbrella.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\qquad$ You do not take an umbrella, or you will stay dry.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ Either you do not take an umbrella, or you will stay dry." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186059415 1074 "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A{:}$ You take an umbrella.\n- $B{:}$ You will stay dry.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A \\Rightarrow B$, which is logically equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\neg A \\lor B\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $\\neg A{:}$ You do not take an umbrella.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\qquad$ You do not take an umbrella, or you will stay dry.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ Either you do not take an umbrella, or you will stay dry." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186059416 1071 "Simplify the statement $\\neg (A \\Rightarrow \\neg B) \\lor A$." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186059416 1071 "Simplify the statement $\\neg (A \\Rightarrow \\neg B) \\lor A.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186059416 1074 "First, note that the conditional statement\n\n$$\nA \\Rightarrow \\neg B\n$$\n\nis logically equivalent to the disjunction\n\n$$\n\\lnot A \\lor \\neg B\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\neg (A \\Rightarrow \\neg B) \\lor A \\equiv \\neg (\\neg A \\lor \\neg B) \\lor A\n$$\n\nNow, we can distribute the negation over the disjunction using De Morgan's law:\n\n$$\n\\neg (\\neg A \\lor \\neg B) \\lor A \\equiv (\\neg (\\neg A) \\land \\neg (\\neg B)) \\lor A\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negations, we reach\n\n$$\n(\\neg (\\neg A) \\land \\neg (\\neg B)) \\lor A \\equiv (A \\land B) \\lor A\n$$\n\nFinally, by the absorption law, we have\n\n$$\n(A \\land B) \\lor A \\equiv A\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $A$." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186059416 1074 "First, note that the conditional statement $A \\Rightarrow \\neg B$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $\\lnot A \\lor \\neg B.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\neg (A \\Rightarrow \\neg B) \\lor A \\equiv \\neg (\\neg A \\lor \\neg B) \\lor A.\n$$\n\nNow, we can distribute the negation over the disjunction using De Morgan's law:\n\n$$\n\\neg (\\neg A \\lor \\neg B) \\lor A \\equiv ( \\neg (\\neg A) \\land \\neg (\\neg B) ) \\lor A\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negations, we reach\n\n$$\n( \\neg (\\neg A) \\land \\neg (\\neg B) ) \\lor A \\equiv (A \\land B) \\lor A.\n$$\n\nFinally, by the absorption law, we have\n\n$$\n(A \\land B) \\lor A \\equiv A.\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $A.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186059417 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $X \\Rightarrow \\neg Y \\equiv \\neg (X \\land Y)$\n\n- $\\neg \\left(X \\Rightarrow \\neg Y\\right)\\equiv \\neg X \\land Y$\n\n- $\\neg \\left(X \\Rightarrow \\neg Y\\right)\\equiv X \\land Y$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186059417 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $X \\Rightarrow \\neg Y \\equiv \\neg (X \\land Y)$\n2. $\\neg \\left(X \\Rightarrow \\neg Y\\right)\\equiv \\neg X \\land Y$\n3. $\\neg \\left(X \\Rightarrow \\neg Y\\right)\\equiv X \\land Y$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186059417 1074 "Let's examine each of our statements:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $X \\Rightarrow \\neg Y$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $\\neg X \\lor \\neg Y$, which by De Morgan's law is equivalent to $\\neg (X\\land Y)$.\n- Statement III is true, while statement II is false. From part I, we have the following equivalence:\n$X \\Rightarrow \\neg Y\\equiv \\neg (X\\land Y)$\nBy negating this statement, we get\n$\\neg \\left(X \\Rightarrow \\neg Y\\right) \\equiv \\neg \\left(\\neg (X\\land Y)\\right)$.\nCanceling out double negation, we reach\n$X\\land Y$.\nSo, $\\neg \\left(X \\Rightarrow \\neg Y\\right)$ is equivalent to $X \\land Y$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186059417 1074 "Let's examine each of our statements:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $X \\Rightarrow \\neg Y$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $\\neg X \\lor \\neg Y,$ which by De Morgan's law is equivalent to $\\neg (X\\land Y).$\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement II is false. From part I, we have the following equivalence:\n\n$$\nX \\Rightarrow \\neg Y\\equiv \\neg (X\\land Y)\n$$\n\nBy negating this statement, we get\n\n$$\n\\neg \\left(X \\Rightarrow \\neg Y\\right) \\equiv \\neg \\left(\\neg (X\\land Y)\\right).\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negation, we reach\n\n$$\nX\\land Y.\n$$\n\nSo, $\\neg \\left(X \\Rightarrow \\neg Y\\right)$ is equivalent to $X \\land Y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186059418 1071 "Find the inverse of the following statement:\n\n$\\qquad$ **If** it is summer, **then** it is not snowy outside." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186059418 1071 "Find the inverse of the following statement: \n\n$\\qquad$ **If** it is summer, **then** it is not snowy outside." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186059418 1074 "In our conditional statement:\n\n- The antecedent is $A{:}$ It is summer.\n\n- The consequent is $B{:}$ It is not snowy outside.\n\nThe inverse of a conditional statement $A \\Rightarrow B$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\neg A \\Rightarrow \\neg B.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the negations of $A$ and $B{:}$\n\n- $\\neg A{:}$ It is not summer.\n\n- $\\neg B{:}$ It is snowy outside.\n\nTherefore, the inverse is the following: \n\n$\\qquad$ If it is not summer, then it is snowy outside." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186059418 1074 "In our conditional statement:\n\n- The antecedent is $A{:}$ It is summer.\n- The consequent is $B{:}$ It is not snowy outside.\n\nThe inverse of a conditional statement $A \\Rightarrow B$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\neg A \\Rightarrow \\neg B\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the negations of $A$ and $B{:}$\n\n- $\\neg A{:}$ It is not summer.\n- $\\neg B{:}$ It is snowy outside.\n\nTherefore, the inverse is the following:\n\n$\\qquad$ If it is not summer, then it is snowy outside." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186059419 1074 "In our conditional statement:\n\n- The antecedent is $A{:}$ It is summer.\n\n- The consequent is $B{:}$ It is not snowy outside.\n\nThe converse of a conditional statement $A \\Rightarrow B$ is given by \n\n$$\nB \\Rightarrow A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the converse is the following: \n\n$\\qquad$ If it is not snowy outside, then it is summer." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186059419 1074 "In our conditional statement:\n\n- The antecedent is $A{:}$ It is summer.\n- The consequent is $B{:}$ It is not snowy outside.\n\nThe converse of a conditional statement $A \\Rightarrow B$ is given by\n\n$$\nB \\Rightarrow A\n$$\n\nTherefore, the converse is the following:\n\n$\\qquad$ If it is not snowy outside, then it is summer." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186059420 1071 "Find the contrapositive of the following statement:\n\n$\\qquad$ **If** it is summer, **then** it is not snowy outside." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186059420 1071 "Find the contrapositive of the following statement: \n\n$\\qquad$ **If** it is summer, **then** it is not snowy outside." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186059420 1074 "In our conditional statement:\n\n- The antecedent is $A{:}$ It is summer.\n- The consequent is $B{:}$ It is not snowy outside.\n\nThe contrapositive of a conditional statement $A \\Rightarrow B$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\neg B \\Rightarrow \\neg A\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the negations of $A$ and $B{:}$\n\n- $\\neg A{:}$ It is not summer.\n- $\\neg B{:}$ It is snowy outside.\n\nTherefore, the contrapositive is the following:\n\n$\\qquad$ If it is snowy outside, then it is not summer." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186059420 1074 "In our conditional statement: \n\n- The antecedent is $A{:}$ It is summer.\n\n- The consequent is $B{:}$ It is not snowy outside.\n\nThe contrapositive of a conditional statement $A \\Rightarrow B$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\neg B \\Rightarrow \\neg A.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the negations of $A$ and $B{:}$\n\n- $\\neg A{:}$ It is not summer.\n\n- $\\neg B{:}$ It is snowy outside.\n\nTherefore, the contrapositive is the following: \n\n$\\qquad$ If it is snowy outside, then it is not summer." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186059421 1071 "$\\qquad$ **If** it is summer, **then** it is not snowy outside.\n\nWhich of the following statements are equivalent to the conditional statement above?\n\n- If it is not summer, then it is snowy outside.\n\n- If it is not snowy outside, then it is summer.\n\n- If it is snowy outside, then it is not summer." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186059421 1071 "$\\qquad$ **If** it is summer, **then** it is not snowy outside.\n\nWhich of the following statements are equivalent to the conditional statement above?\n\n1. If it is not summer, then it is snowy outside.\n2. If it is not snowy outside, then it is summer.\n3. If it is snowy outside, then it is not summer." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186059421 1074 "Let's recall the following facts:\n\n- The contrapositive is logically equivalent to the original statement.\n\n- The inverse and the converse are logically equivalent to each other, but not to the original statement.\n\n![](images/d9/d946d2a81d46cbde934ebbab54f9b69b514092e02da4f8a3b79768f218929449.png)\n\nSo, we are looking for the contrapositive of the given statement.\n\n- Statement I is the inverse, not the contrapositive. $\\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\n- Statement II is the converse, not the contrapositive. $\\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\n- Statement III is the contrapositive. $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186059421 1074 "Let's recall the following facts:\n\n- The contrapositive is logically equivalent to the original statement.\n- The inverse and the converse are logically equivalent to each other, but not to the original statement.\n\n![](images/d9/d946d2a81d46cbde934ebbab54f9b69b514