10 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124596 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362636 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124596 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364572 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124596 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364579 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124605 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364098 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124611 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364626 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124611 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364633 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124620 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362959 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124642 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363061 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124642 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363068 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124642 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363075 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124643 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363034 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124643 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363117 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124644 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363089 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124644 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363110 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124646 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363082 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124646 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363096 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124646 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363103 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124655 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363215 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124655 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363229 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124655 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363236 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124656 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363194 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124656 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364289 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124682 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363892 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124683 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363899 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124684 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363906 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124809 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121421 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186124809 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363041 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124809 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124809 1071 "The function $f(x, y)$ is defined as\n\n$f(x, y) = (\\cos (xy))/(y + 1)$.\nEvaluate this function at the point $(π,-1)$." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186124809 1071 "The function $f(x,y)$ is defined as \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)}{y+1}.\n$$\n\nEvaluate this function at the point $(\\pi ,-1).$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124809 1074 "To evaluate our multivariable function at the point $(\\pi ,-1),$ we substitute $x=\\pi $ and $y=-1$ into the function expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\pi ,-1) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)}{y+1}{|}_{(\\pi ,-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)}{-1+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124864 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121734 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186124864 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363886 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124864 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124864 1071 "$$\n(x-1){y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}}y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The functions $P(x)=\\frac{1}{x-1}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{3}}$ are analytic about $x=1.$\n- The functions $(x-1)P(x)$ and $(x-1)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=1.$\n- The point $x=1$ is an **irregular** singular point of the differential equation." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124864 1071 "$(x - 1)y^{″} + y^{′} + (1)/((x - 1)^{2})y = 0$\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The functions $P(x) = \\frac{1}{x - 1}$ and $Q(x) = (1)/((x - 1)^{3})$ are analytic about $x = 1$.\n2. The functions $(x - 1)P(x)$ and $(x - 1)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x = 1$.\n3. The point $x = 1$ is an **irregular** singular point of the differential equation." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186124864 1074 "A singular point $a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1){y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x-1}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{3}}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Since the denominators of both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ vanish at $x=1,$ they are *not* analytic about $x=1.$ So, statement I is false.\n\n- The function\n\n$$\n(x-1)P(x)=(x-1)\\frac{1}{x-1}=1\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=1,$ while the function\n\n$$\n(x-1)^{2}Q(x)=(x-1)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x-1)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{x-1}\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=1$ since its denominator vanishes at $x=1.$ Therefore, statement II is false.\n\n- Since statement I is false, $x=1$ is a singular point. Also, since statement II is false, it follows that $x=1$ is an **irregular** singular point. Thus, statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124887 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121892 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186124887 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364035 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124887 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124887 1071 "What is the trace in the $yz$-plane of the hyperboloid of one sheet $\\frac{x^{2}}{2} + \\frac{y^{2}}{5} - \\frac{z^{2}}{3} = 1$?" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186124887 1071 "What is the trace in the $yz$-plane of the hyperboloid of one sheet $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{5}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{3}=1?$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124887 1074 "To find the trace in the $yz$-plane, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the hyperboloid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(0)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{5}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{3}=1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{5}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{3}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of the hyperbola in $yz$-plane." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124896 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121946 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186124896 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364048 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124896 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124896 1071 "The level curves of the function $f(x, y) = \\frac{2x^{2} + 1}{x^{2} + y^{2}}$ are given by $f(x, y) = c$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $c = 1$ gives a hyperbola\n2. $c = 2$ gives a parabola\n3. $c = 3$ gives an ellipse" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186124896 1071 "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $c=1$ gives a hyperbola\n- $c=2$ gives a parabola\n- $c=3$ gives an ellipse" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124896 1074 "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =c \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+1 & =c({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) \\\\ (c-2){x}^{2}+c{y}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=1$ then the level curve is ${y}^{2}-{x}^{2}=1,$ which is a hyperbola.\n\n- If $c=2$ then the level curves are $y=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},$ which consists of two lines.\n\n- If $c=3$ then the level curve is ${x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}=1,$ which is an ellipse.\n\nSo, only statements I and III are true." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124918 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122078 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186124918 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364061 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124918 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124918 1071 "In a particular country, there are two groups of people: those who use trains to travel and those who use cars. A study has shown that, each year\n\n- $42%$ of people who used trains swap to cars, and\n- $33%$ of people who used cars swap to trains.\n\nWhich of the following tables describes how these people change their preferences each year?" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186124918 1071 "In a particular country, there are two groups of people: those who use trains to travel and those who use cars. A study has shown that, each year \n\n- $42%$ of people who used trains swap to cars, and\n\n- $33%$ of people who used cars swap to trains.\n\nWhich of the following tables describes how these people change their preferences each year?" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124918 1074 "We are given the following situation for each year: \n\n- $42%$ of people who used trains swap to cars. This implies that $58%$ of them remain using trains.\n\n- $33%$ of people who used cars swap to trains. This implies that $67%$ of them remain using cars.\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Trains** | **Cars** |\n| To: | **Trains** | $0.58$ | $0.33$ |\n| **Cars** | $0.42$ | $0.67$ |  |\n\nNote that we can write a corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0.58 & 0.33 \\\\ 0.42 & 0.67\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124959 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122324 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186124959 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364079 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124959 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124959 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to (-2,3)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy}=$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124959 1071 "What is $lim_(x → (-2, 3))\\frac{2x^{2} + xy^{2}}{x^{2} + 2xy}$?" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186124959 1074 "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(-2,3)$ does not cause the denominator to vanish, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-2,3)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-2,3)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy} & =\\frac{2\\cdot (-2)^{2}+(-2)\\cdot {3}^{2}}{(-2)^{2}+2\\cdot (-2)\\cdot (3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-18}{4-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{-10}{-8} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124991 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122516 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186124991 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364085 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124991 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124991 1071 "Find the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $P(1, 2)$ for the function $f(x, y) = \\frac{1}{y - x^{2}}$." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186124991 1071 "Find the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $P(1,2)$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{y-{x}^{2}}.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186124991 1074 "First, we find the value of the function at $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,2) & =\\frac{1}{2-{1}^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $P(1,2)$ is the $1$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=1.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{1}{y-{x}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ 1 & =y-{x}^{2} \\\\ y & ={x}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $P(1,2)$ is the parabola $y={x}^{2}+1.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186125227 1068 #edb/ref 17592186123932 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186125227 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364317 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186125227 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186125227 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to (0,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{1+xy}-1}=$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186125227 1071 "What is $lim_(x → (0, 1))\\frac{x^{2} - x}{\\sqrt{1 + xy} - 1}$?" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186125227 1074 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{1+xy}-1}=\\frac{{0}^{2}-0}{\\sqrt{1+0\\cdot 1}-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{1+xy}-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,1)}\\frac{({x}^{2}-x)(\\sqrt{1+xy}+1)}{(\\sqrt{1+xy}-1)(\\sqrt{1+xy}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,1)}\\frac{x(x-1)(\\sqrt{1+xy}+1)}{(\\sqrt{1+xy})^{2}-{1}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,1)}\\frac{x(x-1)(\\sqrt{1+xy}+1)}{xy} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,1)}\\frac{(x-1)(\\sqrt{1+xy}+1)}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,1)}\\frac{(x-1)(\\sqrt{1+xy}+1)}{y} & =\\frac{(0-1)(\\sqrt{1+0\\cdot 1}+1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{1} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-2.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186125228 1068 #edb/ref 17592186123938 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186125228 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364323 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186125228 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186125228 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2{y}^{2}x+y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}}=$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186125228 1071 "What is $lim_(x → (0, 0))\\frac{2y^{2}x + yx^{2}}{x^{3} + xy^{2}}$?" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186125228 1074 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2{y}^{2}x+y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot {0}^{2}\\cdot 0+0\\cdot {0}^{2}}{{0}^{3}+0\\cdot {0}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\frac{2{y}^{2}x+y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{3}+{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{2{x}^{3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-x}\\frac{2{y}^{2}x+y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{3}-{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2{x}^{3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2{y}^{2}x+y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186125265 1068 #edb/ref 17592186124160 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186125265 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364647 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186125265 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186125265 1071 "If $C$ is a $3 \\cdot 3$ matrix and $rank(C - 5I) = 3$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $Null(C - 5I) = {0}$\n2. $C - 5I$ is singular\n3. $λ = 5$ is an eigenvalue of $C$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186125265 1071 "If $C$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\text{rank}(C-5I)=3,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{Null}(C-5I)={0}$\n- $C-5I$ is singular\n- $\\lambda =5$ is an eigenvalue of $C$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186125265 1074 "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $3$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(C-5I) & =3-\\text{rank}(C-5I) \\\\  & =3-3 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\text{Null}(C-5I)={0}.$\n\n- Statements II is false. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(C-5I)=0,$ we obtain that $C-5I$ must be non-singular.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that if $M=C-5I$ is non-singular, then $(C-5I)x=0$ has only zero solution.  As a result, $\\lambda =5$ can not be an eigenvalue of $C.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129093 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362518 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129094 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362525 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129094 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364129 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133804 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362993 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133869 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364606 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186134358 1068 #edb/ref 17592186132157 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186134358 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364111 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186134358 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186134358 1071 "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. You're given that the system of equations given below connects the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$  \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{2}+2{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{6}{a}_{3}-4{q}_{1}+{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{12}+{r}_{22}+{r}_{23}?$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186134358 1071 "$[ \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid; a_{1} \\mid a_{2} \\mid a_{3}; \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid ]^(⏞)^(A) = [ \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid; q_{1} \\mid q_{2} \\mid q_{3}; \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid ]^(⏞)^(Q)\\begin{bmatrix}r_{11} & r 12 & r_{13} \\\\ 0 & r 22 & r 23 \\\\ 0 & 0 & r_{33}\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(R)$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. You're given that the system of equations given below connects the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A$.\n\n${q_{1} = \\frac{1}{2}a_{1}; q_{2} = \\frac{1}{4}a_{2} + 2q_{1}; q_{3} = \\frac{1}{6}a_{3} - 4q_{1} + q_{2}$\n\nWhat is the value of $r_{12} + r_{22} + r_{23}$?" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186134358 1074 "Let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{2}+2{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{6}{a}_{3}-4{q}_{1}+{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}=2{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}=-8{q}_{1}+4{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3}=24{q}_{1}-6{q}_{2}+6{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, this system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -8 & 24 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{12}+{r}_{22}+{r}_{23}=-8+4+(-6)=-10.\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186134360 1068 #edb/ref 17592186132169 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186134360 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364117 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186134360 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186134360 1071 "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ \\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖} & \\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖} & \\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above, where ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is an orthogonal set of vectors. You're given that $‖{v}_{1}‖=4,$ $‖{v}_{2}‖=2,$ $‖{v}_{3}‖=6,$ and that the system of equations below describes the relationship between the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$ \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2}={a}_{2}-2{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{3}={a}_{3}-4{v}_{1}+3{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{33}?$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186134360 1071 "$[ \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid; a_{1} \\mid a_{2} \\mid a_{3}; \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid ]^(⏞)^(A) = [ \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid; \\frac{v_{1}}{‖v_{1}‖} \\mid \\frac{v_{2}}{‖v_{2}‖} \\mid \\frac{v_{3}}{‖v_{3}‖}; \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid ]^(⏞)^(Q)\\begin{bmatrix}r 11 & r_{12} & r 13 \\\\ 0 & r_{22} & r_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & r 33\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(R)$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above, where ${v_{1}, v_{2}, v_{3}}$ is an orthogonal set of vectors. You're given that $‖v_{1}‖ = 4$, $‖v_{2}‖ = 2$, $‖v_{3}‖ = 6$, and that the system of equations below describes the relationship between the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A$.\n${v_{1} = a_{1}; v_{2} = a_{2} - 2v_{1}; v_{3} = a_{3} - 4v_{1} + 3v_{2}$\n\nWhat is the value of $r_{11} + r_{13} + r_{33}$?" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186134360 1074 "First, we normalize the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{4}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{1}=4{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{2}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{2}=2{q}_{2} \\\\ {q}_{3} & =\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{3}}{6}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{3}=6{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the set ${{q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}}$ will be both orthogonal and normalized.\n\nNext, let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2}={a}_{2}-2{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{3}={a}_{3}-4{v}_{1}+3{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}={v}_{1}=4{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}=2{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=8{q}_{1}+2{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3}=4{v}_{1}-3{v}_{2}+{v}_{3}=16{q}_{1}-6{q}_{2}+6{q}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{33}=4+16+6=26.\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186147516 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363950 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186147516 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363957 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186147517 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363964 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186147797 1068 #edb/ref 17592186144486 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186147797 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363942 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186147797 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186147797 1071 "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n8{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+10x{y}^{′}+(x-1)y=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a Frobenius series solution to the differential equation, where ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants determined by substitution?\n\n*Hint: Substituting a trial Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =8({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 10x{y}^{′} & =10\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }10(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ (x-1)y & =-{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n-1}-{a}_{n}){x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186147797 1071 "Consider the following differential equation.\n$8x^{2}y^{″} + 10xy^{′} + (x - 1)y = 0$\nWhich of the following is a Frobenius series solution to the differential equation, where $a_{n}$ for $n = 0, 1, 2, …$ are constants determined by substitution?\n\n*Hint: Substituting a trial Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain*\n$8x^{2}y^{″}|= 8(λ^{2} - λ)a_{0}x^{λ} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)8((λ + n)^{2} - (λ + n))a_{n}x^{λ + n},; 10xy^{′}|= 10λa_{0}x^{λ} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)10(λ + n)a_{n}x^{λ + n},; (x - 1)y|=-a_{0}x^{λ} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(a_{n - 1} - a_{n})x^{λ + n}$." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186147797 1074 "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as power series with the $n=0$ term isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =8({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 10x{y}^{′} & =10\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }10(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\ (x-1)y & =-{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n-1}-{a}_{n}){x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 1**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+10\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (8{\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +10\\lambda -1){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (8{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -1){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(8{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -1){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (8{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -1){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(8{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -1){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 8{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can determine a solution. Solving the indicial equation for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -1 & =0 \\\\ (4\\lambda -1)(2\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the two Frobenius series solutions are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1/2}\\,\\text{and}\\,y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the constants ${a}_{n}$ are to be determined separately in each of the two cases.\n\nOf the given options, the correct answer is\n$y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/4}.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186152298 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364364 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186152298 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364565 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186152300 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363816 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186152300 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363836 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186152648 1068 #edb/ref 17592186150365 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186152648 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364143 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186152648 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186152648 1071 "Given that $y_{1}(x) = x^{3}$ is a solution to the equation $x^{2}y^{″} - 5xy^{′} + 9y = 0$ for $x > 0$, use reduction of order to find a second solution $y_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186152648 1071 "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{3}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-5x{y}^{′}+9y=0$ for $x>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186152648 1074 "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){x}^{3}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{x}^{3}+3u{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux)-5x({u}^{′}{x}^{3}+3u{x}^{2})+9(u{x}^{3}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+6{u}^{′}x+6u-5{u}^{′}x-15u+9u) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+{u}^{′}x) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{4}({u}^{″}x+{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{4}\\ne 0$ for $x>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}x+{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}x+w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{x}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{x}\\,⟹\\,u={c}_{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{x}^{3}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}({c}_{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})={x}^{5}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{x}^{3}+B{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157009 1074 "First, let's rewrite the phrase \"the car is fancy and big\" as the equivalent phrase \"the car is fancy and the car is big.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"The car is fancy and the car is big, and the car is big.\"\n\nThen, using the associative law, we can move the comma:\n\n\"The car is fancy, and the car is big and the car is big.\"\n\nNow, we can simplify the phrase \"the car is big and the car is big\" to just \"the car is big.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"The car is fancy, and the car is big.\"\n\nFinally, we can write the sentence more concisely as\n\n\"The car is fancy and big.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157009 1074 "First, let's rewrite the phrase \"the car is fancy and big\" as the equivalent phrase \"the car is fancy and the car is big.\" The sentence now reads\n\n> \"The car is fancy and the car is big, and the car is big.\"\n\nThen, using the associative law, we can move the comma:\n\n> \"The car is fancy, and the car is big and the car is big.\"\n\nNow, we can simplify the phrase \"the car is big and the car is big\" to just \"the car is big.\" The sentence now reads\n\n> \"The car is fancy, and the car is big.\"\n\nFinally, we can write the sentence more concisely as\n\n> \"The car is fancy and big.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186157010 1071 "Simplify $(P \\land Q) \\land (Q \\lor Q)$." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186157010 1071 "Simplify $(P \\land Q) \\land (Q \\lor Q).$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157010 1074 "Using the associative and idempotent laws, we get\n\n$$\n(P∧Q)∧(Q∨Q) \\mid ≡(P∧Q)∧Q; ≡P∧(Q∧Q); ≡P∧Q\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186157010 1074 "Using the associative and idempotent laws, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(P∧Q)∧(Q∨Q) & ≡(P∧Q)∧Q \\\\  & ≡P∧(Q∧Q) \\\\  & ≡P∧Q.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157011 1074 "Using the commutative law, we can replace the phrase \"the table is brown and it is high\" with the phrase \"the table is high and it is brown.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"The table is high and it is brown, and it is brown.\"\n\nThen, using the associative law, we can move the comma:\n\n\"The table is high, and it is brown and it is brown.\"\n\nNow, we can simplify the phrase \"it is brown and it is brown\" to just \"it is brown.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"The table is high and it is brown.\"\n\nFinally, we can write the sentence more concisely:\n\n\"The table is high and brown.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157011 1074 "Using the commutative law, we can replace the phrase \"the table is brown and it is high\" with the phrase \"the table is high and it is brown.\" The sentence now reads\n\n> \"The table is high and it is brown, and it is brown.\"\n\nThen, using the associative law, we can move the comma:\n\n> \"The table is high, and it is brown and it is brown.\"\n\nNow, we can simplify the phrase \"it is brown and it is brown\" to just \"it is brown.\" The sentence now reads\n\n> \"The table is high and it is brown.\"\n\nFinally, we can write the sentence more concisely:\n\n> \"The table is high and brown.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186157012 1071 "Simplify $(A \\land B) \\land (B \\land A)$." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186157012 1071 "Simplify $(A \\land B) \\land (B \\land A).$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157012 1074 "Using the associative, commutative, and idempotent laws, we get\n\n$$\n(A∧B)∧(B∧A) \\mid ≡A∧(B∧B)∧A; ≡A∧B∧A; ≡(A∧A)∧B; ≡A∧B\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186157012 1074 "Using the associative, commutative, and idempotent laws, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A∧B)∧(B∧A) & ≡A∧(B∧B)∧A \\\\  & ≡A∧B∧A \\\\  & ≡(A∧A)∧B \\\\  & ≡A∧B.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157084 1071 "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $X$?\n\n1. $X \\land (Y \\lor X)$\n2. $(X \\lor Y) \\land Y$\n3. $(X \\lor Y) \\land X$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186157084 1071 "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $X?$\n\n- $X \\land (Y \\lor X)$\n\n- $(X \\lor Y) \\land Y$\n\n- $(X \\lor Y) \\land X$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157084 1074 "The law that conjunction absorbs disjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nX \\land (X \\lor Y) \\equiv X\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is logically equivalent to $X$. We have\n$X∧(Y∨X) \\mid ≡X∧(X∨Y); ≡X.✓$\n- Statement II is *not* logically equivalent to $X$. We have\n$(Y∨X)∧Y \\mid ≡Y∧(Y∨X); ≡Y≢X.\\cdot$\n- Statement III is logically equivalent to $X$. We have\n$(X∨Y)∧X \\mid ≡X∧(X∨Y); ≡X.✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186157084 1074 "The law that conjunction absorbs disjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nX \\land (X \\lor Y) \\equiv X.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is logically equivalent to $X.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X∧(Y∨X) & ≡X∧(X∨Y) \\\\  & ≡X.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is *not* logically equivalent to $X.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(Y∨X)∧Y & ≡Y∧(Y∨X) \\\\  & ≡Y≢X.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is logically equivalent to $X.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(X∨Y)∧X & ≡X∧(X∨Y) \\\\  & ≡X.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157085 1071 "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $C$?\n\n1. $(C \\land B) \\lor C$\n2. $B \\lor (B \\land C)$\n3. $C \\lor (C \\lor B)$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186157085 1071 "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $C?$\n\n- $(C \\land B) \\lor C$\n\n- $B \\lor (B \\land C)$\n\n- $C \\lor (C \\lor B)$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157085 1074 "The law that disjunction absorbs conjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nC \\lor (C \\land B) \\equiv C\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is logically equivalent to $C$. We have\n$(C∧B)∨C \\mid ≡C∨(C∧B); ≡C.✓$\n- Statement II is *not* logically equivalent to $C$. We have\n$B∨(B∧C) \\mid ≡B≢C.\\cdot$\n- Statement III is *not* logically equivalent to $C$. This statement cannot be simplified using the absorption laws.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186157085 1074 "The law that disjunction absorbs conjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nC \\lor (C \\land B) \\equiv C.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is logically equivalent to $C.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(C∧B)∨C & ≡C∨(C∧B) \\\\  & ≡C.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is *not* logically equivalent to $C.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B∨(B∧C) & ≡B≢C.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is *not* logically equivalent to $C.$ This statement cannot be simplified using the absorption laws.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157245 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362882 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157245 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362889 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157245 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364537 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157246 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362833 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157246 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362868 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157246 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364544 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157247 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362875 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157247 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364516 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157247 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364558 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157248 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362896 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157248 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362903 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157248 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364551 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157280 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362469 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186157280 1038 #edb/ref 17592