66914 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363790 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186166914 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363803 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186166961 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363856 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186166961 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363883 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186166962 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363863 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186166962 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363870 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186166962 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363877 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186167075 1068 #edb/ref 17592186163498 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186167075 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362677 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186167075 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186167075 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$, away from a fixed origin $O$. When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $xm$, its velocity is $vm/s$ and its acceleration is $(2 + 3e^{-x})m/s^{2}$. If $v = 2m/s$ when $x = 0m$, find the velocity of $P$ when $x = 3m$. Round your answer to one decimal place.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186167075 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x,$ away from a fixed origin $O.$ When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $(2+3{e}^{-x})\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $v=2\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ find the velocity of $P$ when $x=3\\,\\text{m}.$ Round your answer to one decimal place." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186167075 1074 "![](images/ec/ec4af1357e75dcee6d7d5412467c2ee2ea9e54fa38fb6e83817b0ae909c529aa.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(2+3{e}^{-x})\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=2+3{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2+3{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2+3{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=2x-3{e}^{-x}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=2\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2} & =2(0)-3{e}^{-0}+C \\\\ 2 & =-3+C \\\\ C & =2+3 \\\\ C & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2x-3{e}^{-x}+5 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =4x-6{e}^{-x}+10 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{4x-6{e}^{-x}+10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv=\\sqrt{4x-6{e}^{-x}+10}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x=3$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\sqrt{4(3)-6{e}^{-3}+10} \\\\ v & =\\sqrt{22-6{e}^{-3}} \\\\ v & ≈4.7\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186167076 1068 #edb/ref 17592186163504 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186167076 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362683 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186167076 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186167076 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $xm$, the magnitude of its acceleration is $2e^{-x}m/s^{2}$ and is directed toward $O$. Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O$ and has velocity $v = 2m/s$. Find $x$ as a function of time $t$." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186167076 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,\\text{m},$ the magnitude of its acceleration is $2{e}^{-x}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed toward $O.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O$ and has velocity $v=2\\,m/s.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186167076 1074 "![](images/ad/ad437638f22672a10a183f1521929637f8d43725f37a9736c569516232e7c468.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $2{e}^{-x}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-2{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-2{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -2{e}^{-x}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2{e}^{-x}+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=0\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=2\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $K,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2{e}^{-x}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2} & =2{e}^{0}+K \\\\ K & =2-2 \\\\ K & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2{e}^{-x} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =4{e}^{-x} \\\\ v & =\\pm 2{e}^{-x/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=2{e}^{-x/2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=2{e}^{-x/2}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2{e}^{-x/2} \\\\ {e}^{x/2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2 \\\\ \\int {e}^{x/2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int {e}^{x/2}\\text{d}x & =\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ 2{e}^{x/2} & =2t+{C}_{1} \\\\ {e}^{x/2} & =t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=\\frac{{C}_{1}}{2}.$ \n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{e}^{x/2}=t+1.\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x/2} & =t+1 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x/2}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1) \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1) \\\\ x & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186167105 1068 #edb/ref 17592186163678 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186167105 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362973 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186167105 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186167105 1071 "Find the maximum value of $Q(x) = x_{1}^{2} + 4x_{1}x_{2} + x_{2}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖ = 1$." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186167105 1071 "Find the maximum value of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186167105 1074 "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}=3.\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186167318 1068 #edb/ref 17592186165087 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186167318 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364105 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186167318 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186167318 1071 "If $\\,u(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{7t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,v(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]\\,$ are solutions to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$, find $Aw(t)$ where $w(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{7t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186167318 1071 "If $u(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{7t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $v(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ e^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}$ are solutions to $x^{′}(t) = Ax(t)$, find $Aw(t)$ where $w(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{7t} \\\\ e^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186167318 1074 "Since $u(t)$ and $v(t)$ are solutions to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ the superposition principle guarantees that \n\n$$\ncu(t)+dv(t)\n$$\n\n is also a solution to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for all $c,d\\in R.$\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\nw(t)=u(t)+v(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $w$ is a solution to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t).$ So, in order to find $Aw(t)$ we just need to find ${w}^{′}(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Aw & ={w}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}({e}^{7t})^{′} \\\\ ({e}^{2t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7{e}^{7t} \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186167366 1068 #edb/ref 17592186165374 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186167366 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364586 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186167366 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186167366 1071 "$\\int \\frac{7}{25-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186167366 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int\\frac{7}{25 - x^{2}}dx$?" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186167366 1074 "Let's rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{7}{25-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{7}{25}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{x}{5})}^{2}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{5})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{5}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{5}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=5\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{7}{25-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{7}{25}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{x}{5})}^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{25}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{5}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{1-{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{5}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{5}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this result is subject to the condition $|\\frac{x}{5}|<1.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186172053 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364653 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186172054 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364660 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186172055 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364667 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186172055 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364674 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186172667 1068 #edb/ref 17592186171597 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186172667 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364620 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186172667 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186172667 1074 "A curve is closed if its terminal point coincides with its initial point.\n\nOf the given options, the only that satisfies the definition is the following:\n\n![](images/e9/e90eb350064508e6afdc2517b37faa903b75f0370067be8fac4428e9294cd990.png)" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186175933 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362744 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186175933 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362751 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186175933 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364013 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186175933 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364016 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186175958 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364019 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186175958 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364440 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186175958 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364523 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186175959 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364007 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186175959 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364022 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186175959 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364025 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186176440 1068 #edb/ref 17592186174790 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186176440 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363637 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186176440 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186176440 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t$ is given by $v(t) = 12t^{2} - 4t$. What is the total displacement of the particle between $t = 0$ and $t = 3$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186176440 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t$ is given by $v(t)=12{t}^{2}-4t.$ What is the total displacement of the particle between $t=0$ and $t=3?$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186176440 1074 "We find the total displacement by calculating the definite integral of the velocity.\n\nLet $d$ be the total displacement. Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(12{t}^{2}-4t)\\,\\text{dt} \\\\  & ={(4{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2})|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =[4(3)^{3}-2(3)^{2}]-[4(0)^{3}-2(0)^{2}] \\\\  & =[108-18]-0 \\\\  & =90.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the total displacement between $t=0$ and $t=3$ is $90.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186176582 1068 #edb/ref 17592186175600 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186176582 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364434 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186176582 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186176582 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t \\ge 0$ is given by $v(t) = e^{t/3} - 1$. At time $t = 0$ the particle is located at the position $x = e + 1$. What is the position of the particle at time $t = 3$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186176582 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t\\ge 0$ is given by $v(t)={e}^{t/3}-1.$ At time $t=0$ the particle is located at the position $x=e+1.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=3?$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186176582 1074 "The position of the particle at $t=3$ is equal to its position at $t=0$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [0,3].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(3)=x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{3}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(3) & =(e+1)+{\\int }_{0}^{3}({e}^{t/3}-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =e+1+{[3{e}^{t/3}-t]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =e+1+([3{e}^{(3)/3}-(3)]-[3{e}^{(0)/3}-(0)]) \\\\  & =e+1+([3e-3]-[3]) \\\\  & =e+1+3e-6 \\\\  & =4e-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=3$ is $x=4e-5.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186179437 1071 "Consider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx = u - v - 2, \\qquad y = v - u + 5.\n$$\n\nSuppose that $A$ is the matrix representing the linear transformation associated with $\\mathbf T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ![](images/b3/b3d58dc3ce18b0f5c977b930088152bb7b9c4c821edb656bc478904b67ae8acf.png)\n\n- $\\det(A) = 0$\n\n- The transformation is invertible" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186179437 1071 "Consider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx = u - v - 2, \\qquad y = v - u + 5.\n$$\n\nSuppose that $A$ is the matrix representing the linear transformation associated with $\\mathbf T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\n- $\\det(A) = 0$\n\n- The transformation is invertible" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186179437 1074 "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v-2 \\\\ v-u+5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v \\\\ -u+v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\det(A) = 0,$ the affine transformation is *not* invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186179437 1074 "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\