athbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $\\mathbf{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $\\mathbf T$ gives us the following information:\n\n![](images/ea/ead1edec5d6fa82de8eb0137a838826221e0f45093782bc1b0a32111b05a37f4.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(-4)\\cdot 7-(-9)\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 9 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 9 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c = -7 + 3 + 8 = 4.\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186179438 1074 "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $\\mathbf{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $\\mathbf T$ gives us the following information:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(-4)\\cdot 7-(-9)\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 9 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 9 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c = -7 + 3 + 8 = 4.\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186179442 1071 "![](images/87/8797a50ddc0750a97190f96d8bc1cffe70c6ef9cc8e55a46f5a75d6b6702c81d.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nDescribe the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $\\mathbf{T}.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186179442 1071 "![](images/87/8797a50ddc0750a97190f96d8bc1cffe70c6ef9cc8e55a46f5a75d6b6702c81d.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n![](images/34/347a44c388bd81e78d4dcf5f40584ce76273ffe25513ebb1522568ba479ac4fc.png) \nDescribe the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $\\mathbf{T}.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186179442 1074 "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbf{u} \\longrightarrow A \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{b},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\mathbf u$ and $\\mathbf b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-singular, i.e., when $\\det(A) \\neq 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular, i.e., when $\\det(A) = 0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $\\mathbf{T}$ is \n![](images/94/94625329d7eba1da88a9dde36b6e64335a9801658a8c1fd054cea9db835ea4fa.png)\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2-4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-invertible and the image of the triangle under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is a line segment." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186179442 1074 "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbf{u} \\longrightarrow A \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{b},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\mathbf u$ and $\\mathbf b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-singular, i.e., when $\\det(A) \\neq 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular, i.e., when $\\det(A) = 0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $\\mathbf{T}$ is \n$[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2-4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-invertible and the image of the triangle under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is a line segment." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186179776 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362778 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186179776 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362785 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186179776 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362792 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186179777 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362799 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186179777 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362806 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186179778 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362813 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186179998 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177627 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186179998 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362772 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186179998 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186179998 1071 "Which of the following affine transformations are invertible?\n\n- ${T}_{1}:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}v-u \\\\ u+v\\end{aligned}]$\n- ${T}_{2}:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}u-2 \\\\ u-v+1\\end{aligned}]$\n- ${T}_{3}:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3u+6v \\\\ u+2v-1\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186179998 1071 "Which of the following affine transformations are invertible?\n\n1. $T_{1}: \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}v - u \\\\ u + v\\end{bmatrix}$\n2. $T_{2}: \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u - 2 \\\\ u - v + 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n3. $T_{3}: \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}3u + 6v \\\\ u + 2v - 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186179998 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nThe transformation is invertible if the matrix $A$ is invertible. That is, if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's look at each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{1}$ is invertible. Notice that ${T}_{1}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}v-u \\\\ u+v\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-u+v \\\\ u+v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|=(-1)\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 1=-2\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is nonzero, ${T}_{1}$ is invertible.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{2}$ is invertible. Notice that ${T}_{2}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u-2 \\\\ u-v+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ u-v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|=1\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot 1=-1\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is nonzero, ${T}_{2}$ is invertible.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{3}$ is not invertible. Notice that ${T}_{3}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3u+6v \\\\ u+2v-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3u+6v \\\\ u+2v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|=3\\cdot 2-6\\cdot 1=0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is zero, ${T}_{3}$ is *not* invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${T}_{1}$ and ${T}_{2}$ only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186184070 1071 "Find $C^5$ given that\n![](images/46/46d9e424cedc06da6c58d5219fc0161b29da17c75fa9dc714e758a0e3280b52a.png)" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186184070 1071 "Find $C^5$ given that\n![](images/c7/c757e46b9d91c0f57e72fd05d2b5d83558332b6e20fcefdf75d9f0388796da05.png)" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186184165 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362931 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186184165 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362938 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186184165 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364378 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186184166 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363660 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186184166 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363667 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186184166 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364385 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186192770 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364233 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186192770 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364240 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186192770 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364247 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186192770 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364254 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186192771 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362559 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186192771 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362566 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186192773 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363145 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186192773 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363152 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186192792 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363138 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186192792 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363159 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186192793 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364261 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186192808 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363124 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186192808 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363131 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186193329 1068 #edb/ref 17592186191871 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186193329 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364067 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186193329 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186193329 1071 "Which of the following sets are neither open nor closed?\n\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0\\le x<2,\\,0<y<2}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<2,}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{2},\\,-1\\le x\\le 1}$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186193329 1071 "Which of the following sets are neither open nor closed?\n\n1. ${(x, y) ∈ R^{2}: 0 \\le x < 2, 0 < y < 2}$\n2. ${(x, y) ∈ R^{2}: x^{2} + y^{2} < 2,}$\n3. ${(x, y) ∈ R^{2}: 0 \\le y \\le x^{2},-1 \\le x \\le 1}$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186193329 1074 "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(0,1).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(1,0).$\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The third set is *closed* since it contains all its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186193330 1068 #edb/ref 17592186191877 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186193330 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364073 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186193330 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186193330 1071 "Consider the set $A$, defined by\n$A = {(x, y) ∈ R^{2}: 0 \\le x \\le 2,-1 \\le y < 0}$.\n\nThe interior of $A$ is given by\n$intA = {(x, y) ∈ R^{2}: 0 < x < 2, A A A A A}$.\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186193330 1071 "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,-1\\le y<0}.\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0<x<2,\\,\\,}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186193330 1074 "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nThe set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/fc/fc71859cb4d1e58ea3d15c2da66bce014898281fe237f2e0c2396f96c84c9e79.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/ef/eff037b96b6a42120fdfa112a98d1e0749cdd56bd89acd647db448b08d7323ad.png)\n\nSo, in this case, the interior of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0<x<2,\\,-1<y<0\\,}.\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186198448 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363243 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186198450 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363250 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186198450 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363257 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186221459 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363732 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186221460 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363766 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186222007 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220684 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186222007 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220687 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186222007 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220690 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186222007 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363744 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186222007 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363747 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186222007 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363750 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186222007 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363754 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186222007 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363759 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186222007 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363763 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186222007 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186222007 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs above, where $t\\in R.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The function $a(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} \\\\ {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}]$  \n{{field-1}}  \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ The function $b(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]$  \n{{field-2}}  \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ The function $c(t)=[\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{3t} \\\\ 5{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}]$  \n{{field-3}}  \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ {{field-4}}  \ndoes not give a set of fundamental solutions to the system because the Wronskian  \n\n$$\nW({{field-5}})={{field-6}}\n$$\n\n$\\,$ for all $t\\in R.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186222007 1071 "${x_{1}^{′}(t) = 2x_{1}(t) + x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = x_{1}(t) + 2x_{2}(t)$.\nConsider the system of linear ODEs above, where $t ∈ R$. Select the correct options in the reasoning below. The function $a(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{3t} \\\\ e^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}$\n{{field-1}}\na solution of the system.\n\nThe function $b(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{t} \\\\ e^{t}\\end{bmatrix}$\n{{field-2}}\na solution of the system.\n\nThe function $c(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}5e^{3t} \\\\ 5e^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}$\n{{field-3}}\na solution of the system." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186222007 1074 "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent. In this case, they do **not** form a fundamental set of solutions.\n\nFirst, we write our system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t).\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute the given vectors into the matrix differential equation:\n\n- $a(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} \\\\ {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}]$ $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}({e}^{3t})^{′} \\\\ ({e}^{3t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} \\\\ {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{3t} \\\\ 3{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {e}^{3t}+1\\cdot {e}^{3t} \\\\ 1\\cdot {e}^{3t}+2\\cdot {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{3t} \\\\ 3{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{3t} \\\\ 3{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $b(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]$ $\\text{isn't}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}({e}^{t})^{′} \\\\ ({e}^{t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {e}^{t}+1\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\ 1\\cdot {e}^{t}+2\\cdot {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{t} \\\\ 3{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $c(t)=[\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{3t} \\\\ 5{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}]$ $\\text{is}$ also a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(5{e}^{3t})^{′} \\\\ (5{e}^{3t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{3t} \\\\ 5{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}15{e}^{3t} \\\\ 15{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 5{e}^{3t}+1\\cdot 5{e}^{3t} \\\\ 1\\cdot 5{e}^{3t}+2\\cdot 5{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}15{e}^{3t} \\\\ 15{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}15{e}^{3t} \\\\ 15{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, consider the set of solutions ${a,c}.$ Its Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(a,c) & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & 5{e}^{3t} \\\\ {e}^{3t} & 5{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =({e}^{3t})(5{e}^{3t})-(5{e}^{3t})({e}^{3t}) \\\\  & =5{e}^{6t}-5{e}^{6t} \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is zero for all $t\\in R.$ Thus, since $a$ and $c$ are both solutions of the same linear system, but their Wronskian is identically zero, they are linearly dependent and do **not** form a fundamental set of solutions of the system." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186228951 1071 "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\nx^2y'' + x(x-4)y' - 4y = 0\n$$\n\nThe general solution near $x=0$ of the differential equation is given by\n\n$$\ny = c_1y_1(x) + c_2y_2(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $c_1$ and $c_2$ are arbitrary constants and $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are linearly independent solutions. According to the Frobenius method, what is the best choice for the first solution $y_1,$ where $a_n$ for $n=0,1,2,\\ldots$ are constants determined by substitution?\n\n*Hint: You may assume $x=0$ is a regular singular point of the ODE and the indicial equation of the ODE is $\\lambda^2-5\\lambda+4=0.$*" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186228951 1071 "Consider the following differential equation.\n$x^2y'' + x(x-4)y' - 4y = 0$\nThe general solution near $x=0$ of the differential equation is given by\n$y = c_1y_1(x) + c_2y_2(x)$,\nwhere $c_1$ and $c_2$ are arbitrary constants and $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are linearly independent solutions. According to the Frobenius method, what is the best choice for the first solution $y_1$, where $a_n$ for $n=0,1,2,\\ldots$ are constants determined by substitution?\n\n*Hint: You may assume $x=0$ is a regular singular point of the ODE and the indicial equation of the ODE is $\\lambda^2-5\\lambda+4=0$.*" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [1