{x}^{2/3}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} \\\\  & ={x}^{2/3}({a}_{0}+\\frac{4}{5}{a}_{0}x+\\frac{1}{5}{a}_{0}{x}^{2}+⋯) \\\\  & ={a}_{0}({x}^{2/3}+\\frac{4}{5}{x}^{5/3}+\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{8/3}+⋯).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362462 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362463 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362462 1069 "q-102738" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362462 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362462 1071 "Simplify the statement $¬(¬Q⇒¬P).$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362462 1072 #uuid "800902e0-4fe9-58b3-806d-c353ad7c0574" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362462 1074 "First, note that\n\n$$\n¬(¬Q⇒¬P)≡¬(¬(¬Q)∨¬P).\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negations, we reach\n\n$$\n¬(¬(¬Q)∨¬P)≡¬(Q∨¬P).\n$$\n\nWe can distribute the negation over the disjunction using De Morgan's law:\n\n$$\n¬(Q∨¬P)≡¬Q∧¬(¬P).\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negations, we reach\n\n$$\n¬Q∧¬(¬P)≡¬Q∧P.\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $P∧¬Q.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362463 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362464 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362463 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362465 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362463 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362466 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362463 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362467 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362463 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362468 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362463 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362463 1077 "selection" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362463 1079 #edb/ref 17592186362465 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362463 1080 #uuid "4a2fd454-ba38-57fd-a0ef-9a04661c9580" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362464 1081 "¬P∨¬Q" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362464 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362464 1084