592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362778 1071 "Which of the following affine transformations are invertible?\n\n- ${T}_{1}:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}u+v+6 \\\\ 7v-2u+6\\end{aligned}]$\n- ${T}_{2}:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2u+v-2 \\\\ 4+4u+2v\\end{aligned}]$\n- ${T}_{3}:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}u+9 \\\\ 8u+7v+6\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362778 1072 #uuid "ad1b6191-80b0-5b35-b5bb-b0b81838cf26" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362778 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nThe transformation is invertible if the matrix $A$ is invertible. That is, if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's look at each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{1}$ is invertible. Notice that ${T}_{1}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u+v+6 \\\\ 7v-2u+6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u+v \\\\ -2u+7v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{aligned}|=7\\cdot 1-1\\cdot (-2)=9\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is nonzero, ${T}_{1}$ is invertible.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{2}$ is not invertible. Notice that ${T}_{2}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2u+v-2 \\\\ 4+4u+2v\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2u+v \\\\ 4u+2v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{ali