363152 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363152 1071 "Which of the following sets are open?\n\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }0<x<3,\\,0<y<6-2x}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }1<x<4,\\,x<y<{x}^{2}}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le {y}^{2}\\le 5}$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363152 1072 #uuid "be4d8952-950b-5fb0-a574-0337f64e1d84" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363152 1074 "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The third set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363153 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363154 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363153 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363155 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363153 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363156 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363153 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363157 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363153 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363158 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363153 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363153 1077 "selection" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363153 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363158 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363153 1080 #uuid "39d81623-879e-5