he set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/88/88f35519cf1aa01bcc9e4fb91b24c2bc2a1a823867ec2670563b0b45f2643a0a.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/64/649c33f1738a35f3d298038bd2cf05d3b435424a4efbbb1e6143cf0d56b2bfd6.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A\\cup ∂A=A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. In this case, the set $A$ contains all of its boundary points, so we must have $A⊈\\text{int}A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $A-\\text{int}A=∂A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363160 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363161 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363160 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363162 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363160 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363163 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363160 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363164 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363160 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363165 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363160 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363160 1077 "selection" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363160 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363162 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363160 1080 #uuid "6419e7cc-8024-5bbd-a920-bed8d2a11d14" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363161 1081 "None of the statements are true" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363161 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533445 17592186045