on of the corresponding homogeneous equation), and\n\n- ${y}_{p}(x)$ is a particular solution of the original inhomogeneous equation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check which of the two given functions is a particular solution of our inhomogeneous equation:\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)={x}^{2},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2})^{′}=2x, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(2x)^{′}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}(2)-6x(2x)+10({x}^{2})=-2{x}^{3}.\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)={x}^{2}$ $\\text{isn't}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)={x}^{3},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{3})^{′}=3{x}^{2}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(3{x}^{2})^{′}=6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}(6x)-6x(3{x}^{2})+10({x}^{3})=-2{x}^{3}.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)={x}^{3}$ $\\text{is}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\nWe are told that the complementary solution of our equation is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{x}^{5}+B{x}^{2}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{x}^{5}+B{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A\\text{ and }B$ are arbitrary constants." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363837 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363838 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363837 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363839 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363837 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363840 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363837 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363841 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363837 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363837 1077 "field-4" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363837 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363839 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363837 1080 #uuid "ae623db1-b3ed-5159-b65c-fdd456f3ac5b" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363838 1081 "C{x}^{2}" 1319413953