he differential equation." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363906 1072 #uuid "eb08b8c8-dd65-588a-9aea-12dc17027ceb" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363906 1074 "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nA singular point $x=a$ is regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x+1)(x-2)^{3}(x-4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)(x-2)^{3}(x-4){y}^{″}+(x-2){y}^{′}+(x+1)(x-4)y=0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{(x+1)(x-2)^{2}(x-4)}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{3}}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+1)(x-2)^{2}(x-4)}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nThe function $P(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $(x+1)(x-2)^{2}(x-4)=0,$ which occurs at $x=-1,$ $x=2,$ and $x=4.$\n\nThe function $Q(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $(x-2)^{2}=0,$ which occurs at $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, $x=0$ is $\\text{an ordinary}$ point of the differential equation, and the singular points are\n\n$$\nx=-1,\\,x=2,\\,x=4.\n$$\n\nNow, let's classify each singular point.\n\n- For $x=-1,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x+1)P(x)=(x+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x+1)(x-2)^{2}(x-4)}=\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}(x-4)},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=-1,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x+1)^{2}Q(x)=(x+1)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x-2)^{3}},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=-1.$ Therefore, $x=-1$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=2,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x-2)P(x)=(x-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x+1)(x-2)^{2}(x-4)}=\\frac{1}{(x+1)(x-2)(x-4)},\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=2.$ So,  $x=2$ is an $\\text{an irregular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=4,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x-4)P(x)=(x-4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x+1)(x-2)^{2}(x-4)}=\\frac{1}{(x+1)(x-2)^{2}},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=4,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x-4)^{2}Q(x)=(x-4)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x-2)^{3}},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=4.$ Therefore, $x=4$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363907 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363908 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363907 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363909 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363907 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363910 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363907 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363907 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363907 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363910 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363907 1080 #uuid "a8afcc8e-65ec-5987-9e41-a2fb2dd85e87" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363908 1081 "an irregular singular" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363908 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363908 1084 #uuid "1552ce05-7007-5e91-8a50-e8d37165557f" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363909 1081 "an ordinary" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363909 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363909 1084 #uuid "3786ad73-cf93-5149-97a0-bee76391f86b" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363910 1081 "a regular singular" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363910 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363910 1084 #uuid "c7fe661d-c81a-5b5f-9a49-cf2079e56e8a" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363911 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363912 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363911 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363913 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363911 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363914 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363911 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533445 17592186045585 tru