\\infty }{a}_{n}({\\lambda }_{2}){x}^{n}.$\n\n- **Case 2**: If ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2},$ then\n${y}_{2}(x)={y}_{1}(x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{{\\lambda }_{1}}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}{x}^{n}.$\n\n- **Case 3**: If ${\\lambda }_{1}-{\\lambda }_{2}\\in N,$ then\n${y}_{2}(x)={b}_{-1}{y}_{1}(x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{{\\lambda }_{2}}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}{x}^{n}.$\n\nWe are given that the indicial equation is $4{\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +3=0.$\nSolving for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +3 & =0 \\\\ (2\\lambda -1)(2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe larger solution is ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\frac{3}{2}\\ge \\frac{1}{2}={\\lambda }_{2}.$ Therefore, the first Frobenius series solution we take is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{3/2}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nNow, since ${\\lambda }_{1}-{\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}=1$ *is* a positive integer, the other solution is given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={b}_{-1}{y}_{1}(x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{1/2}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere the constants ${b}_{n}$ are found by substitution." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363936 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363937 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363936 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363938 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363936 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363939 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363936 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363940 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363936 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363941 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363936 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363936 1077 "selection" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363936 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363941 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363936 1080 #uuid "9f47b069-4588-575d-a356-3b581235302b" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363937 1081 "{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{1