ambda +n})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\ {y}^{″} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Rewrite all the series in the equation so that they use the same index of summation, the powers of $x$ are the same, and the indices start at the same value.\n\nThe terms of the differential equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x{y}^{″} & =6x\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }6((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ 5{y}^{′} & =5\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }5(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ -y & =-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the $y$ term, we change the index of the summation by writing $n=m-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n-y=-{\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{m-1}{x}^{\\lambda +m-1}.\n$$\n\nThen, replacing $m$ with $n$ (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n-y=-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, isolating the $n=0$ terms for the other terms in the ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x{y}^{″} & =6({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ 5{y}^{′} & =5\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }5(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+5\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} & = \\\\ (6{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +5\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} & = \\\\ (6{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lam