lution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & ={x}^{1/3}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} \\\\  & ={x}^{1/3}({a}_{0}+{a}_{0}x+\\frac{2}{7}{a}_{0}{x}^{2}+⋯) \\\\  & ={a}_{0}({x}^{1/3}+{x}^{4/3}+\\frac{2}{7}{x}^{7/3}+⋯).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363996 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363997 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363996 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363996 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363996 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363997 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363996 1080 #uuid "fa03ce68-d947-52e5-b148-2e538eeab9ea" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363997 1081 "\\frac{2}{7}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363997 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363997 1084 #uuid "756ef84c-123e-55e2-8259-2f38c43864f0" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363998 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363999 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363998 1069 "q-345708" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363998 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363998 1071 "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\nx{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+4y=0\n$$\n\nThe general solution near $x=0$ of the differential equation is given by\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are arbitrary constants and ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent solutions. According to the Frobenius method, what is the missing coefficient in the first solution ${y}_{1}?$\n\n*Hint: You may assume $x=0$ is a regular singular point of the ODE, the indicial equation of the ODE is ${\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda =0,$ and the recurrence relation for the coefficients is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}(\\lambda )=-\\frac{4}{(\\lambda +n)((\\lambda +n)-3)}\\,{a}_{n-1}.\n$$\n\n*\n\nThe first solution is \n\n${y}_{1}(x)={a}_{0}({x}^{3}-{x}^{4}\\,+$ {{field-1}} ${x}