5492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364005 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364005 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364006 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364005 1080 #uuid "2772ad5e-c448-56fd-8938-23c472bdd89d" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364006 1081 "-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364006 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364006 1084 #uuid "9dc096a5-2a92-5c1a-a917-6f73d7bce0ed" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364007 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364008 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364007 1069 "q-348324" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364007 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364007 1071 "![](images/77/77accc09333167b5d086558ab56120a240f31e03c9c575a22d55f4d76b6f7c45.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the velocity of an object moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis. At time $t=1,$ the particle is located at the position $x=2.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=6?$\n\nThe position of the particle at $t=6$ is $x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364007 1072 #uuid "a2ff4ec4-ed5e-5c71-be84-c469e5bd9304" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364007 1074 "The position of the particle at $t=6$ is equal to its position at $t=1$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [1,6].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(6)=x(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{6}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nWe are told that $x(1)=2$, and we can compute the integral ${\\int }_{1}^{6}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ by finding the signed area under the graph from $t=1$ to $t=6.$ Computing the signed area, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{6}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx(6)=2+\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=6$ is $x=\\frac{7}{2}.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364008 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364009 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364008 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364008 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364008 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364009 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364008 1080 #uuid "8337ac9c-f456-5764-8e64-63e79e1abaa4" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364009 1081 "7/2" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364009 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364009 1084 #uuid "b652457f-bbe7-586b-9f3f-3e4aa605d828" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364010 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364011 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364010 1069 "q-350235" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364010 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364010 1071 "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n${\\int }_{2}^{5}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n$\\operatorname{ln}⁡(${{field-1}}$)$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364010 1072 #uuid "ddc73c8e-5882-5ea3-8d97-cfcb89e490de" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364010 1074 "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic cosine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $(2x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=2x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $4$ | $10$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{4}^{10}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{10}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡u\\,{|}_{4}^{10} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(10)-\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(4) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10+\\sqrt{{(10)}^{2}-1})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\sqrt{{(4)}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10+\\sqrt{99})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\sqrt{15}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{10+\\sqrt{99}}{4+\\sqrt{15}})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364011 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364012 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364011 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364011 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364011 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364012 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364011 1080 #uuid "8e37d524-66cb-51ab-9745-41c1884781f2" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364012 1081 "\\frac{10+\\sqrt{99}}{4+\\sqrt{15}}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364012 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364012 1084 #uuid "78303239-9dfa-50d4-9f1e-ff3706d288ae" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364013 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364014 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364013 1069 "q-351419" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364013 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364013 1071 "A particle $Q$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t$ is given by $v(t)=-3{t}^{2}+6t.$ Find the total displacement $d$ of the particle between $t=0$ and $t=2.$\n\n$d=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364013 1072 #uuid "75facc73-2630-5d6f-8c66-273fb7236531" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364013 1074 "We find the total displacement by calculating the definite integral of the velocity.\n\nLet $d$ be the total displacement between $t=0$ and $t=2.$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(-3{t}^{2}+6t)\\,\\text{dt} \\\\  & =(-{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}){|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[-(2)^{3}+3(2)^{2}]-[-(0)^{3}+3(0)^{2}] \\\\  & =[-8+12]-0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364014 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364015 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364014 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364014 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364014 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364015 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364014 1080 #uuid "8d3e5e05-c21b-5573-a9a7-4254df7613d1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364015 1081 "4" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364015 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364015 1084 #uuid "6e61ed37-bf6c-55e0-9d4b-b89fb19280a9" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364016 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364017 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364016 1069 "q-351423" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364016 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364016 1071 "A particle $Q$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t$ is given by $v(t)=-4(t-1)^{3}.$ Find the total displacement $d$ of the particle between $t=1$ and $t=3.$\n\n$d=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364016 1072 #uuid "90c184be-aaf1-517b-836b-b1dc7bd713f3" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364016 1074 "We find the total displacement by calculating the definite integral of the velocity.\n\nLet $d$ be the total displacement between $t=1$ and $t=3.$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(-4(t-1)^{3})\\,\\text{dt} \\\\  & =(-(t-1)^{4}){|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =[-{(3-1)}^{4}]-[-{(1-1)}^{4}] \\\\  & =-16-0 \\\\  & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364017 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364018 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364017 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364017 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364017 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364018 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364017 1080 #uuid "6bc00e9c-1fab-5b7a-aab5-0ed085747f89" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364018 1081 "-16" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364018 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364018 1084 #uuid "037f8dc6-a757-523d-9d6c-8a06be5adf1b" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364019 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364020 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364019 1069 "q-351464" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364019 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364019 1071 "A particle $Q$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t\\ge 0$ is given by $v(t)={e}^{t}+3.$ At time $t=0$ the particle is located at the position $x=-{e}^{2}.$ \nFind the position of the particle at time $t=2.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe position of the particle at $t=2$ is $x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364019 1072 #uuid "fe02dfee-2fec-57c1-ab9d-76d8ccae719c" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364019 1074 "The position of the particle at $t=2$ is equal to its position at $t=0$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [0,2].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(2)=x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{2}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(2) & =-{e}^{2}+{\\int }_{0}^{2}({e}^{t}+3)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{e}^{2}+{[{e}^{t}+3t]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-{e}^{2}+({e}^{2}+3(2))-({e}^{0}+3(0)) \\\\  & =-{e}^{2}+({e}^{2}+6)-(1+0) \\\\  & =-{e}^{2}+({e}^{2}+5) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the position of the particle at $t=2$ is $x=5.$" 13194139533445 