77" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364129 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364130 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364129 1069 "q-48110" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364129 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364129 1071 "![](images/c6/c6746e568dbd7df0064db959a6ee43b4f86994400348c84eb2f7c8e259bdf1dc.png)\n\nFor which points does the function $y=f(x)$ above have a jump discontinuity?" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364129 1072 #uuid "3f45c575-223b-54cd-917e-cd28ef5e84a9" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364129 1074 "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(-2)=3$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)=3,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)=4.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-2$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nThe function also has a jump discontinuity at $x=3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(3)=2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)=2,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=3$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there is also a discontinuity at $x=1,$ but it is not a jump discontinuity.\n\nHence, the correct answer is $x=-2$ and $x=3$ only." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364130 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364131 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364130 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364132 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364130 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364133 1319413953