37 1081 "x=e-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364437 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364437 1084 #uuid "60524c21-f88c-51dd-ac68-ed9c161c025d" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364438 1081 "x=4e-5" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364438 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364438 1084 #uuid "c5c41551-bc04-5069-aa5f-127c65b1dd1b" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364439 1081 "x=3{e}^{3}-e" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364439 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364439 1084 #uuid "7a6b53d4-511d-5fac-bfb2-21928289e6c3" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364440 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364441 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364440 1069 "q-81507" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364440 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364440 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t>0$ is given by $v(t)={e}^{2-t}.$ At time $t=1$ the particle is located at the position $x=e.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=2?$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364440 1072 #uuid "dfa9f5d6-79ff-5f17-8761-60660e8e1882" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364440 1074 "The position of the particle at $t=2$ is equal to its position at $t=1$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [1,2].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(2)=x(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{2}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(2) & =e+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{e}^{2-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =e+{[-{e}^{2-t}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =e+([-{e}^{2-2}]-[-{e}^{2-1}]) \\\\  & =e+([-{e}^{0}]-[-{e}^{1}]) \\\\  & =e+(-1+e) \\\\  & =2e-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=2$ is $x=2e-1.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364441 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364442 1319413