\frac{{x}^{2}+xy+x+y}{xy+{y}^{2}}.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364530 1072 #uuid "fb25e06c-c7b0-5dbe-83ae-8f7b0c5dbf13" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364530 1074 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xy+x+y}{xy+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{(-1)^{2}+(-1)\\cdot 1+(-1)+1}{(-1)\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xy+x+y}{xy+{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x(x+y)+(x+y)}{y(x+y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{(x+1)(x+y)}{y(x+y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x+1}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x+1}{y} & =\\frac{(-1)+1}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $0.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364531 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364532 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364531 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364533 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364531 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364534 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364531 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364535 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364531 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364536 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364531 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364531 1077 "selection" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364531 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364535 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364531 1080 #uuid "b0f7b683-d099-548b-9b98-dddfeba7f07a" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364532 1081 "DNE" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364532 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364532 1084